\subsection*{Derivation bridges}

\begin{lemma}[Adiabatic invariant along a path]
\label{lem:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:adiabatic_invariant_along_path}
\lean{IPhO2026_3_B_2.adiabatic_invariant_along_path}
\uses{def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:ParamagneticTorusLaws, def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:IsAdiabaticPath, def:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:adiabaticInvariant}
Under the governing laws and the adiabatic first-law balance, any two states
of one adiabatic path share the same value of $T^2\,(\lambda + \mu_0 K H^2)$.
This is the carrier of the integration step
$dT/T = -(\mu_0 K/\lambda)\,H\,dH$.
Its derivability rests on the path-differentiability fields of the laws:
off a differentiable path the first-law balance is vacuous (both
$\mathrm{d}/\mathrm{d}t$ read as junk $0$) and the claim is false --- the
Review countermodel class of iter-011 --- so the regularity premise is
load-bearing, not cosmetic.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Differentiate the equation of state along the differentiable path
(product rule, legitimate by the \texttt{temp\_differentiable} and
\texttt{mag\_differentiable} fields): $V\,(T\,\dot M + M\,\dot T) = nK\,\dot H$.
The work rate is $w = \mu_0 V H\,\dot M$; substituting
$V\,\dot M = nK\,\dot H/T - V M\,\dot T/T$ (from the differentiated EOS) into
the first-law balance $C_M\,\dot T = -w$ with $C_M = n\lambda/T^2$ and
$M = nKH/(TV)$ gives
$(n\lambda/T^2)\,\dot T = -\mu_0 H(nK\,\dot H/T - nKH\,\dot T/T^2)$.
Cancelling $n > 0$ and multiplying by $T^2$:
$(\lambda + \mu_0 K H^2)\,\dot T + \mu_0 K T H\,\dot H = 0$, equivalently
$\dot T/T = -(\mu_0 K/\lambda)\,H\,\dot H$.  Hence the derivative of
$t \mapsto T(t)^2(\lambda + \mu_0 K H(t)^2)$ vanishes at every parameter by
the product rule, using only the proved positivity
$\lambda + \mu_0 K H^2 > 0$ of
\cref{lem:IPhO2026Problems_problem_IPhO_2026_3_B_2:lam_add_mu0_K_sq_pos};
an everywhere differentiable function with identically vanishing derivative
is constant (mean value theorem), so the invariant agrees at $t_1$ and $t_2$.
\end{proof}

